The legal test you are referring to is the "Divergenzkontrolle" or "Divergence Control" test, which is applied by the German Federal Fiscal Court (Bundesfinanzhof, BFH) to determine whether a case should be admitted for review due to a divergence from existing jurisprudence regarding the uniform application of tax law.

The test is based on § 115 Abs. 2 Nr. 2 Alt. 2 FGO (Finanzgerichtsordnung, Fiscal Court Procedure Act). The elements of the test are:

1. A previous decision by the BFH or another higher court (Oberfinanzgericht or Bundesfinanzhof) has already ruled on the same or a similar issue;
2. The decision to be reviewed deviates from this previous decision;
3. The deviation is relevant to the outcome of the case.

The leading authority for this test is the decision of the BFH, dated 10 February 1999 (Aktenzeichen: XI R 77/97). However, I must note that I am unsure if this is the most recent or only leading authority on this matter, as German law is subject to continuous development and refinement.
